# MZ Logistics - Global Rules

You are working on the MZ Logistics digital freight and transportation platform.

Always follow these rules throughout the project.

## General Development

- Write clean, readable, maintainable, production-quality code.
- Follow SOLID principles where appropriate.
- Follow DRY, KISS, and separation of concerns.
- Avoid unnecessary complexity and over-engineering.
- Do not create large files or components when they can be logically separated.
- Reuse existing components, services, utilities, models, and patterns before creating new ones.
- Use clear and descriptive naming for variables, methods, classes, files, routes, APIs, and database fields.
- Never use vague names such as data1, temp, item2, testValue, etc.
- Keep business logic separate from UI code.
- Avoid duplicated business logic.
- Do not hardcode business rules, statuses, URLs, API endpoints, colors, strings, or configuration values.
- Use constants, enums, configuration files, localization files, or environment variables as appropriate.
- Never make unrelated changes while implementing a requested feature.
- Before modifying existing functionality, understand its current flow and dependencies.
- Preserve backward compatibility unless a breaking change is explicitly required.
- Do not delete existing functionality unless explicitly instructed.

## Architecture

- Maintain clear separation between presentation, business logic, data access, and infrastructure.
- Every feature should have a clear responsibility.
- Prefer feature-based modular architecture.
- Keep modules loosely coupled.
- Shared functionality must live in shared/common/core modules.
- Avoid circular dependencies.
- Design the system so features can grow without requiring major rewrites.

## MZ Logistics Business Domain

Use consistent domain terminology across all projects:

- Customer
- Service Provider
- Driver
- Shipment Request
- Quotation
- Job
- Trip
- Truck
- Equipment
- Payment
- Invoice
- Settlement
- Proof of Delivery (POD)

Important business hierarchy:

Customer creates Shipment Request
→ Service Providers submit Quotations
→ Customer selects a Quotation
→ Payment
→ Job is created
→ Job contains one or multiple Trips
→ Each Trip is assigned to a Truck and Driver
→ Trip execution and tracking
→ Proof of Delivery
→ Job completion
→ Financial settlement

Important:

- A Driver belongs to a Service Provider.
- A Truck belongs to a Service Provider.
- Drivers and trucks must not be treated as independent companies or service providers.
- A Job may contain multiple Trips.
- Trip and Job are separate entities and must never be treated as the same thing.

## UI / UX

All interfaces must look professionally designed by a real product design team.

The design must be:

- Modern
- Clean
- Professional
- Minimal
- Elegant
- Business-oriented
- Logistics-oriented
- Easy to understand
- Visually balanced

Avoid generic AI-generated looking interfaces.

Do NOT:

- Overuse gradients.
- Overuse glassmorphism.
- Use excessive rounded cards everywhere.
- Add decorative elements without purpose.
- Use random icons.
- Use excessive shadows.
- Create huge headings unnecessarily.
- Fill every screen with cards.
- Use unrealistic placeholder dashboards.
- Add random statistics or fake data without a functional purpose.

Prefer:

- Strong visual hierarchy.
- Proper whitespace.
- Clear typography.
- Consistent spacing.
- Subtle borders.
- Meaningful icons.
- Professional tables.
- Clear forms.
- Practical dashboards.
- Logical information grouping.

## Responsive Design

Every interface must be fully responsive.

Support:

- Mobile
- Tablet
- Laptop
- Desktop
- Large desktop screens

Do not simply shrink desktop UI for mobile.

Design responsive layouts properly.

For mobile:
- Simplify navigation.
- Stack content intelligently.
- Avoid horizontal overflow.
- Keep touch targets accessible.

For desktop:
- Use available space efficiently.
- Use appropriate tables, grids, sidebars, filters, and dashboards.

## Arabic and English

The entire system must support:

- Arabic
- English

Arabic:
- RTL
- Arabic-friendly typography using IBM Plex Sans Arabic for all languages
- Correct alignment
- Correct icon direction where applicable

English:
- LTR

Never hardcode visible UI text.

All user-facing strings must come from localization files.

Changing language must update:

- Text
- Direction
- Alignment
- Navigation direction
- Icons when direction-sensitive
- Forms
- Tables
- Dialogs
- Date display where applicable

The interface must remain visually correct in both RTL and LTR.

## Forms

Forms must have:

- Clear labels.
- Proper validation.
- Helpful error messages.
- Required field indicators.
- Loading states.
- Disabled states.
- Success feedback.
- Error feedback.

Do not rely only on placeholders as labels.

Prevent duplicate submissions.

## States

Every data-driven page must handle:

- Loading
- Empty
- Error
- Success
- No permission
- Offline/network failure where applicable

Never leave users with an infinite loader.

## Accessibility

- Maintain readable font sizes.
- Maintain sufficient contrast.
- Use accessible touch/click targets.
- Support keyboard navigation on web where applicable.
- Do not communicate important state using color alone.
- Add semantic labels where applicable.

## Security

- Never expose secrets or credentials in source code.
- Use environment variables.
- Never trust client-side validation alone.
- Always enforce authorization on the backend.
- Do not expose internal database IDs or sensitive information unnecessarily.
- Validate and sanitize input.
- Follow least-privilege permissions.

## API Integration

- Centralize API configuration.
- Centralize authentication handling.
- Handle token expiration consistently.
- Handle API errors consistently.
- Do not call APIs directly from random UI components.
- Use typed request and response models where supported.
- Avoid duplicated API calls.
- Prevent unnecessary repeated network requests.

## Git / Code Quality

Before considering a task complete:

- Remove debug code.
- Remove unused imports.
- Remove commented-out code that is no longer needed.
- Remove temporary logs.
- Ensure formatting is correct.
- Ensure naming is consistent.
- Check for obvious regressions.
- Run relevant tests or static analysis where available.

Do not create commits unless explicitly requested.